### When `Scanner` Is Too Slow: High-Performance I/O

While `Scanner` is convenient, it can be too slow for competitive programming or processing large files (e.g. $10^5$ to $10^6$ entries) due to regex parsing overhead and synchronized small buffers (1024 chars).

#### The `BufferedReader` Solution
`BufferedReader` reads characters into a large buffer (default **8192 characters**), making it 5x to 10x faster:

```java
import java.io.BufferedReader;
import java.io.InputStreamReader;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.util.StringTokenizer;

public class FastIOExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
        BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(System.in));

        // Read full line as text
        String line = reader.readLine();

        // Tokenize space-separated values
        StringTokenizer tokenizer = new StringTokenizer(line);
        int a = Integer.parseInt(tokenizer.nextToken());
        int b = Integer.parseInt(tokenizer.nextToken());

        System.out.println("Sum: " + (a + b));
    }
}
```

#### Key Comparisons:
| Feature | `Scanner` | `BufferedReader` |
|---|---|---|
| Buffer Size | 1 KB | 8 KB |
| Parsing | Built-in regex (`nextInt()`, etc.) | Reads Strings only (`Integer.parseInt()` needed) |
| Speed | Slower | Much faster |
| Exception Handling | Unchecked (`InputMismatchException`) | Checked (`throws IOException`) |

